('`<span class="kicker">Tablet l. ${LINE_NUMS[li]}, term ${si + 1}</span>', '`<span class="kicker">泥板第 ${LINE_NUMS[li]} 行，第 ${si + 1} 个术语</span>'),
('`<span class="kicker">Colophon</span><p style="margin:6px 0 8px">“This (is) a song (<i>zammārum</i>) of the <i>nid qibli</i>, a <i>zaluzi</i> […]; hand of Ammurabi.” <i>Nid qibli</i> is <i>nīd qablim</i>, the tuning. Ammurabi is the scribe; Kilmer restores a composer, Urḫiya, who is named on h.8, h.11 and h.13 (Appendix B). Koitabashi compares Mesopotamian hymn colophons, which also give a genre and then the copyist’s name.</p>',
 '`<span class="kicker">题记</span><p style="margin:6px 0 8px">“这是 <i>nid qibli</i> 的一首歌（<i>zammārum</i>），一首 <i>zaluzi</i> […]；出自 Ammurabi 之手。”<i>Nid qibli</i> 即调律 <i>nīd qablim</i>。Ammurabi 是书吏；Kilmer 在残缺处补出一位作曲者 Urḫiya，他的名字见于 h.8、h.11 和 h.13（附录 B）。Koitabashi 拿它与美索不达米亚颂歌的题记相比，那些题记同样先写体裁，再写抄写者的名字。</p>'),
